On Sat, 10 Oct 2026 12:19:38 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> Probably we can detect when patches have been applied, and respond with
> the specific SHA they've been applied as. This mirrors the FreeBSD
> practice of updating PRs with a message like "a commit in main
> references this bug."
>
> Is this something we can do deterministically and/or with inference? I
> would prefer to not have to reference the message ID in every commit I
> make.

Both. The deterministic part covers most cases and needs nothing in
your commits.

The change id doesn't work for this. git am drops it, and none of the
patch change ids I checked show up on trunk. The applied commits got
new ones.

The diff does work. git patch-id --stable hashes only the diff, so a
patch and the commit it became get the same id. That holds through
git am, a rebase, or a reworded message. I posted this as
"[PATCH] mail: Add applied.sh to find patches trunk applied" [1].
Give it a trunk range and it prints "<message-id> <commit>" for each
patch on the list that the range applied. Over the last 40 trunk
commits it found the three patches you applied unchanged, including
"[PATCH] zib: Add hello-lab-devel" [2], whose message you reworded.

It misses a patch you edit before applying, because the edit changes
the diff. "[PATCH 1/2] claude: Reply to all in process-email" [3] is
one: it landed as adfead7e7960 with a different patch-id. That's
where inference comes in. I can match those by subject, author, and
content, but that is a judgment call, so the reply should say so.

Here is how I'd connect it, if you agree. I haven't built this part.
At the start of each process-email pass, after the fetch, I'd run
applied.sh over the trunk commits that are new since the last pass.
For each patch it finds, I'd reply "Applied to trunk as <sha>". For a
patch I match only by inference, I'd reply "Applied with edits as
<sha>" and give my reasons. The replies come from me, so you write
nothing, and they live on the list as the record.

> The goal is to clearly indicate on the list which patches have been
> committed, so I can take them out of the review queue automatically.

With those replies on the list, mail-tag.sh can derive an "applied"
tag from them, the same way it derives "replied". The review queue
search then excludes tag:applied. Archiving stays your call, since
only the moderator archives.

Two questions before I build the rest:

1. Should the deterministic replies and the inferred ones look
   different, e.g. different first lines, so the tag counts only the
   sure matches? Or do you want both counted?

2. Should "applied" be tagged on the patch alone, or on the whole
   series once every patch in it has landed?

